/// \defgroup grp_w3_module_xfer Module transfer chain and save helpers
/// \ingroup grp_save_load_crc
/// \brief How object and drawable modules save, load and checksum
///        themselves: the base-class transfer chain, a set of module
///        transfer functions built on it, the object-id list helper and
///        the LatchRestore scope guard.
///
/// Every module attached to an object or a drawable is a Snapshot, so it
/// has one transfer function, xfer, that serves saving, loading and the
/// checksums alike (\ref sub_save_load_crc_xfer). Module classes form a
/// hierarchy, and each level's xfer calls its parent's, so the record a
/// module leaves in a save is every level's version number and fields, in
/// the order the functions call each other. The functions in this group
/// are mostly short; their value is in where they sit in that chain and in
/// the save-format rules they show.
///
/// \par The base of the chain in BFME 2
/// From the matched bodies:
/// - ObjectModule::xfer is the root on the object side. It transfers two
///   version numbers and no fields.
/// - BehaviorModule::xfer transfers its version, then calls
///   ObjectModule::xfer.
/// - UpdateModule::xfer transfers its version and calls
///   BehaviorModule::xfer. Then, unless the stream is computing the light
///   CRC, it transfers one 32-bit field (in Zero Hour, the frame and phase
///   of the module's next update) and, when loading, resets another field
///   to -1.
/// - UpgradeModule::xfer transfers its version, calls BehaviorModule::xfer
///   and then %UpgradeMux::upgradeMuxXfer, which transfers a version and one
///   value (in Zero Hour, whether the upgrade has already been applied).
/// - On the drawable side, DrawModule::xfer calls the same body as
///   ObjectModule::xfer, then transfers a version of its own.
///
/// Concrete modules add their own version and fields on top, as the
/// functions below show. Zero Hour orders a transfer as version, then base
/// class, then fields. Several BFME 2 modules call the base class first or
/// last instead (SlaughterHordeContain::xfer calls it last), so the order
/// must be read from each body and cannot be assumed.
///
/// \par Versions
/// An Xfer::Version holds two numbers: the earliest version the class can
/// still read and its current version. Only the current version is stored,
/// as one byte. When loading, the stored byte replaces the current one, so
/// after the call the version object holds the version found in the
/// stream; a transfer function then skips the fields that older saves do
/// not have. A stored version older than the earliest or newer than the
/// current one throws an XferException. Xfer::Version1 is the shortcut for
/// a class at version 1 that reads only version 1.
///
/// \par The light CRC
/// Xfer::IsLightCRC reports that a stream is computing the light checksum,
/// the cheaper form of the logic CRC (\ref sub_save_load_crc_crc). In that
/// mode Object::xfer transfers only a short summary of the object (among
/// other things its template name, id, health, an AI summary and its
/// weapons) and returns before its behavior-module loop. Through
/// Object::xfer, object module records therefore reach saves and the full
/// form of the logic CRC, where IsLightCRC is false, but not the light
/// form. Drawable modules are not reached by the logic CRC in either
/// form. Some functions in this group still test IsLightCRC and return at
/// once or skip their own fields; that would matter only to a caller that
/// transfers a module in light-CRC mode, and no such caller is
/// established.
///
/// \par Shared retail bodies
/// Many module transfer functions are byte-for-byte identical (a version,
/// then the same base-class call), and the retail linker kept one copy for
/// all of them. The ledger names each shared copy after one class, so a
/// caller listed for it may really be calling another class's identical
/// function. Functions below note sharing where the project's records
/// show it.
///
/// \par Where module records sit in a save
/// Object::xfer transfers an object's behavior modules among its own
/// fields: a count, then for each module its module tag name and the
/// module's record wrapped in a block. When loading, each record is
/// matched to a module by that tag, and a record whose tag the object no
/// longer has is skipped as a block. A module the object has but the save
/// does not mention is simply not transferred.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Which module classes an object or drawable carries is decided by its
///   INI definition: ModuleFactory creates each module from the name it
///   registered for that class (for example SlaughterHordeContain). None of
///   the transfer functions here read INI values.
/// - Because records are matched by module tag, renaming a module's tag in
///   an object's INI definition makes saves written before the change drop
///   that module's saved state on load (Zero Hour: the tag is the name
///   written after the module class on the module's INI line).
/// - Object modules are transferred with their object. When
///   %GameLogic::getCRC hashes the objects in the full form of the logic CRC,
///   Object::xfer hashes each behavior module's record too, so changing
///   what an object module transfers can change that checksum. In
///   light-CRC mode module records are skipped (see The light CRC above).
///   Which form a given multiplayer check uses is not established here
///   (\ref sub_save_load_crc_crc). Either way every machine in a game must
///   run the same module code, because the simulation itself must agree.
///   Drawable modules such as W3DPropDraw are client state: saved, but not
///   in the logic CRC.
/// - Adding a saved field to a module without breaking existing saves
///   follows the pattern SlaughterHordeContain::xfer uses: raise the
///   current version, keep the earliest, and transfer the new field only
///   when the loaded version is new enough. Removing or reordering fields,
///   or dropping a version-only level of the chain, breaks loading older
///   saves that contain that module.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// - A save stores memory images: a version byte per level, 32-bit
///   integers and floats as their four bytes, strings with a length
///   prefix. It also carries framing around the values, which includes
///   the following: each behavior-module record that Object::xfer writes
///   is wrapped in a block (a marker and the offset of the block's end),
///   and in tagged streams every value is preceded by its type tag. The
///   stream header and the rest of the framing are described in
///   \ref sub_save_load_crc_xfer. Reading original saves on another
///   platform means reproducing that framing as well as the values,
///   including the version-only levels that carry no data.
/// - Replay files do not contain these records; a replay is re-simulated
///   from commands (\ref sub_save_load_crc_replays). Module records do
///   reach the full form of the logic CRC, so changing what a transfer
///   function transfers changes that checksum. Whether, and how, BFME 2
///   compares logic CRCs while a replay plays is not established here.
///
/// \see \ref sub_save_load_crc, \ref sub_objects_modules


/// \fn void ObjectModule::xfer(Xfer *xfer)
/// \ingroup grp_w3_module_xfer
/// \brief Transfers the base of every object module's saved record: two
///        version numbers and no data.
///
/// This is where the object-side transfer chain ends. Callers in the
/// retail call graph include BehaviorModule::xfer, through which the
/// behavior-side chains (UpdateModule::xfer and UpgradeModule::xfer among
/// them) reach it, and DrawModule::xfer. The transfer functions of
/// LaserUpdate, SwayClientUpdate, BeaconClientUpdate,
/// EvaAnnounceClientCreate and RandomSoundSelectorClientBehavior call it
/// directly too; in Zero Hour the first three are client update modules.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Calls Xfer::Version1 twice. Zero Hour's ObjectModule::xfer transfers
/// its own version 1 and then calls Module::xfer, which transfers another
/// version 1. The BFME 2 body makes the same two version transfers,
/// consistent with Module::xfer having been inlined; whether BFME 2 still
/// has a separate Module level is not established.
///
/// \param xfer The stream that saves, loads or checksums the module.
///
/// \note In Zero Hour the drawable-side modules (DrawModule and the client
///       update modules) derive from DrawableModule, not ObjectModule, and
///       DrawableModule::xfer has the same source as this function. That
///       the BFME 2 drawable-side transfers call this body is consistent
///       with the linker having merged two identical functions; whether
///       BFME 2 also changed the class hierarchy is not established.
/// \par Remastering
/// The two version bytes carry no data, but a port that reads original
/// saves must still read them for every module.
/// \see BehaviorModule::xfer, DrawModule::xfer, Xfer::Version1


/// \fn void NotifyTargetsOfImminentProbableCrushingUpdate::xfer(Xfer *xfer)
/// \ingroup grp_w3_module_xfer
/// \brief Transfers a NotifyTargetsOfImminentProbableCrushingUpdate module:
///        its version, the update-module base and its embedded helper.
///
/// NotifyTargetsOfImminentProbableCrushingUpdate is a BFME update module
/// with no Zero Hour counterpart. Going by its name, it warns objects that
/// a crushing object is about to run them over. Its update,
/// NotifyTargetsOfImminentProbableCrushingUpdate::update, hands the work to
/// a helper object embedded in the module, passing it the owning object
/// and a value from the module's data; the helper also decides when the
/// module runs next.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - Transfers version 1 (earliest version 1).
/// - Calls UpdateModule::xfer, which brings in the rest of the base chain.
/// - Calls the embedded helper's own transfer function, which transfers
///   only a version number of its own.
///
/// The module therefore saves no state beyond the base chain and two
/// version numbers. There is no light-CRC check at this level;
/// UpdateModule::xfer applies its own.
///
/// \param xfer The stream that saves, loads or checksums the module.
///
/// \note The reconstruction's notes on the retail virtual tables record
///       that the horde variant,
///       HordeNotifyTargetsOfImminentProbableCrushingUpdate, uses this same
///       body.
/// \see UpdateModule::xfer,
///      NotifyTargetsOfImminentProbableCrushingUpdate::update


/// \fn void TransportAIUpdate::xfer(Xfer *xfer)
/// \ingroup grp_w3_module_xfer
/// \brief Transfers a TransportAIUpdate module: its version, then the full
///        AI state of AIUpdateInterface.
///
/// TransportAIUpdate adds no saved fields of its own, so its record is a
/// version byte in front of the AI state that AIUpdateInterface::xfer
/// transfers (\ref sub_ai_pathfinding).
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Calls Xfer::Version1, then AIUpdateInterface::xfer. The body is the
/// same as Zero Hour's TransportAIUpdate::xfer.
///
/// \param xfer The stream that saves, loads or checksums the module.
///
/// \note The retail body is shared. The ledger records that the transfer
///       functions of WanderAIUpdate (whose Zero Hour source is the same),
///       HordeAIUpdate and SiegeAIUpdate compile to the same bytes, so one
///       copy serves all four classes. HordeWorkerAIUpdate::xfer calls it
///       as its base-class step; because of the sharing, that call does
///       not show which of the four classes HordeWorkerAIUpdate derives
///       from.
/// \see AIUpdateInterface::xfer


/// \fn void ArmorUpgrade::xfer(Xfer *xfer)
/// \ingroup grp_w3_module_xfer
/// \brief Transfers an ArmorUpgrade module: its version, then the
///        upgrade-module base.
///
/// ArmorUpgrade keeps no saved state of its own. The only state it saves
/// belongs to its UpgradeModule base, which UpgradeModule::xfer transfers
/// together with its upgrade trigger (%UpgradeMux::upgradeMuxXfer).
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Calls Xfer::Version1, then UpgradeModule::xfer. The body is the same as
/// Zero Hour's ArmorUpgrade::xfer.
///
/// \param xfer The stream that saves, loads or checksums the module.
///
/// \note Retail keeps one copy of this body for upgrade modules whose
///       transfer functions compile to identical bytes. The project's
///       symbol pins record StatusBitsUpgrade::xfer at this copy, and
///       StatusBitsUpgradeIfEldestKindof::xfer calls it as its base-class
///       step. Zero Hour's StatusBitsUpgrade::xfer has the same source as
///       this function, so the call most likely reflects the sharing, not
///       derivation from ArmorUpgrade.
/// \see UpgradeModule::xfer


/// \fn void SlaughterHordeContain::xfer(Xfer *xfer)
/// \ingroup grp_w3_module_xfer
/// \brief Transfers a SlaughterHordeContain module: two fields added in
///        version 2, then the HordeGarrisonContain base.
///
/// SlaughterHordeContain is a BFME container module built on
/// HordeGarrisonContain; it is also in BFME 1, and Zero Hour has no
/// counterpart. CitadelSlaughterHordeContain derives from it, and
/// CitadelSlaughterHordeContain::xfer calls this function as its
/// base-class step.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - Returns at once when Xfer::IsLightCRC is true, before the base-class
///   call. Object::xfer does not transfer modules in light-CRC mode at all
///   (see the group's paragraph on the light CRC).
/// - Transfers its version: current 2, earliest 1.
/// - If the version (on load, the one read from the stream) is 2 or
///   later, transfers an unsigned 32-bit value and an AsciiString. Both
///   are members the constructor clears; their meaning is not established
///   here.
/// - Calls HordeGarrisonContain::xfer last, after its own fields. Zero
///   Hour's convention is the reverse: version, base class, then fields.
///
/// Loading a version-1 record leaves the two members at their constructed
/// values.
///
/// \param xfer The stream that saves, loads or checksums the module.
///
/// \par Modding
/// This is the pattern to copy when adding saved state to a module: the
/// version-2 fields are read only from version-2 records, so saves written
/// before they existed still load.
/// \see HordeGarrisonContain::xfer, CitadelSlaughterHordeContain::xfer


/// \fn void W3DPropDraw::xfer(Xfer *xfer)
/// \ingroup grp_w3_module_xfer
/// \brief Transfers a W3DPropDraw draw module: its version, then the
///        draw-module base. There is no data of its own.
///
/// W3DPropDraw is a draw module of the W3D device layer
/// (\ref sub_w3d_rendering). Draw modules belong to drawables, which are
/// client state: they are saved, but they are not in the logic CRC.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Calls Xfer::Version1, then DrawModule::xfer. The body is the same as
/// Zero Hour's W3DPropDraw::xfer, whose source notes that the class has no
/// data to save.
///
/// \param xfer The stream that saves or loads the module.
///
/// \note W3DFloorDraw::xfer calls this body as its base-class step. Draw
///       modules whose transfer functions compile to identical bytes can
///       share one retail copy (in Zero Hour, for example, the transfer
///       functions of W3DDefaultDraw and W3DTreeDraw make the same calls
///       as this function), so the call alone does not show which class
///       W3DFloorDraw derives from.
/// \see DrawModule::xfer, ObjectModule::xfer


/// \fn void DelayedLuaEventList::xfer(Xfer *xfer)
/// \ingroup grp_w3_module_xfer
/// \brief Transfers a fixed list of three delayed Lua event records.
///
/// DelayedLuaEventList is a Snapshot holding three event records, each a
/// Snapshot itself. A DelayedLuaEventUpdate module constructs one as a
/// member, and DelayedLuaEventUpdate::xfer ends by transferring it through
/// this function. The class is BFME-only; BFME 1 has the same class, with
/// three records of the same size.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - In light-CRC mode it returns at once, transferring nothing.
/// - Transfers version 1 (earliest version 1).
/// - Calls each record's own transfer function, in array order.
///
/// No count is stored: all three records are always transferred, used or
/// not.
///
/// \param xfer The stream that saves, loads or checksums the list.
///
/// \par Modding
/// The three-record capacity is fixed in code, not in data. Changing it
/// changes the save format of every object carrying a
/// DelayedLuaEventUpdate module.
/// \see DelayedLuaEventUpdate::xfer


/// \file XferSTLObjectIDList.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w3_module_xfer
/// \brief xferSTLObjectIDList, the transfer helper for lists of object
///        ids.


/// \fn Xfer * xferSTLObjectIDList(Xfer *xfer, BridgeBehaviorObjectIDList *list)
/// \brief Saves, loads or checksums a list of object ids: a version, the
///        element count, then each id.
///
/// Several modules that keep a list of object ids transfer it with this
/// helper; others, such as BridgeBehavior and BroadcastStealthUpdate,
/// transfer their lists inline. Callers of the helper in the retail call
/// graph include BezierProjectileBehavior::xfer,
/// ArrowStormUpdate::xfer, DamageFieldUpdate::xfer,
/// RousingSpeechUpdate::xfer and GloriousChargeUpdate::xfer. The list is
/// a doubly linked list of object ids with a sentinel node, the shape of
/// Zero Hour's `std::list<ObjectID>`; the type name in the
/// reconstruction's signature is a working name.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - Transfers version 1 (earliest version 1).
/// - Passes the string "std::list" to an Xfer virtual function whose role
///   is not yet established; other container helpers pass their own
///   container's name the same way. The element count follows, through
///   the operator that transfers 32-bit unsigned values.
/// - If Xfer::IsStoring reports that the stream is storing, each id in
///   list order goes through XferObjectID, which transfers it with
///   Xfer::XferEnum.
/// - Otherwise the list must be empty. If it is not, the helper throws an
///   XferException with the message "List must be empty on load".
///   Otherwise it reads the stored number of ids and appends each one.
///
/// \param xfer The stream to use.
/// \param list [in,out] The list to transfer. When loading it must be
///             empty and receives the stored ids in their saved order.
/// \return The xfer argument.
///
/// \note Zero Hour's Xfer::xferSTLObjectIDList, whose name this function
///       carries, is a member of Xfer and stores the count as an unsigned
///       16-bit value. The BFME 2 body is a free function and transfers the
///       count through the 32-bit unsigned operator, so the two games'
///       records differ. Zero Hour also sends checksum streams down the
///       storing branch explicitly; in BFME 2 the branch depends only on
///       Xfer::IsStoring.
/// \par Remastering
/// The count is four bytes in a BFME 2 save, not two as in Zero Hour.
/// \see XferObjectID


/// \class LatchRestore
/// \ingroup grp_w3_module_xfer
/// \brief Scope guard that sets a variable for the lifetime of a scope
///        and puts its old value back when the guard is destroyed.
///
/// LatchRestore is a small class template from Zero Hour's
/// Common/LatchRestore.h, parameterised on the type of the variable it
/// guards. Its constructor takes two arguments: dest, the variable to
/// override, and src, the value dest holds until the guard is destroyed.
/// The constructor saves the current value of dest, keeps a reference to
/// dest and assigns src to it. The virtual destructor writes the saved
/// value back, so the override ends however the scope is left, including
/// by an early return or an exception. Because the guard keeps a
/// reference, dest must outlive it.
///
/// %LatchRestoreCtor.cpp instantiates the template for `bool`. The BFME 2
/// binary contains further instantiations, and the ledger names some of
/// them, for example `Team *` and `Player *`, the types Zero Hour's
/// ScriptEngine latches to hold the calling team and the current player.
/// The linker can fold instantiations whose code is identical into one
/// retail copy. When a copy serves several types, the type in its
/// reconstructed name is the project's choice, not something the binary
/// records. The ledger (reverse/functions.csv) and tools/callers_of.py
/// show where each instantiation's functions are reconstructed and which
/// BFME 2 functions call them.
///
/// Zero Hour uses `LatchRestore<Bool>` to hold a flag for the duration of
/// an operation: %GameState::loadGame sets the game state's in-load-game
/// flag while it reads a save, GameLogic::update sets the logic's in-update
/// flag, and %GameLogic::getCRC latches the file-scope inCRCGen flag to
/// true when called outside GameLogic::update and to false when called
/// inside it.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// The constructor and destructor bodies are Zero Hour's and BFME 1's
/// template bodies; the BFME 2 unit takes the `bool` constructor from
/// BFME 1's %GameState.cpp, where the bytes are identical.
/// \see \ref sub_save_load_crc
